# tab_decomp

**Claim carried:** C2/C8 (appendix): where the width gap to the package PPI++ comes from, arm by arm on the same draws.

**Shows:** decomp.csv (3000 draws per cell, one draw shared by all arms): width ratio to the package PPI++ (`ppi-python` 0.2.3, tuning clipped to [0, 1]) = (SD ratio of the point estimates over the draws) x (the arm's SE/SD divided by the package PPI++'s SE/SD; SE/SD = mean estimated standard error over the SD of the estimates); SE/SD below 1 = the estimated standard error is too small. Arms: package PPI++; PPI++ with the population-optimal tuning (`ppi++_oracle_lambda`: no estimation noise in lambda); in-sample OLS slope and cross-fitted linear, pooled; PPI++ on the cross-fitted linear predictor; cross-fitted spline. n = 100, 500 (decomp.csv has more). The two `recentred` arms of decomp.csv equal the package PPI++ to 1e-9 in every cell and are not shown. Longtable.

**How to read:** SD ratio below 1 = the point estimate is less variable than PPI++'s; SE/SD below 1 = the interval is shorter than the spread of the estimate warrants, so read both with coverage: a short interval whose SE/SD is below 1 under-covers.

